   <h1>FAQ — Frequently Asked Questions</h1>

<p>This FAQ covers the most common questions about Desktop‑2FA, vault behavior, security, installation, and CLI usage.</p>

<hr />

<h2>What is Desktop‑2FA?</h2>
<p>Desktop‑2FA is a fully offline TOTP authenticator for developers and power users.  
It stores secrets in an encrypted vault using Argon2id + AES‑256‑GCM.</p>

<hr />

<h2>Where is the vault stored?</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Linux/macOS</strong>: <code>~/.desktop-2fa/vault</code></li>
  <li><strong>Windows</strong>: <code>C:\Users\&lt;username&gt;\.desktop-2fa\vault</code></li>
</ul>

<p>The vault is created automatically when needed.</p>

<hr />

<h2>What happens if the vault does not exist?</h2>

<p>Running commands like <code>d2fa list</code> or <code>d2fa add</code> will:</p>

<ol>
  <li>Show “No vault found.”</li>
  <li>Announce that a new vault will be created.</li>
  <li>Prompt for password + confirmation.</li>
  <li>Create the vault explicitly.</li>
</ol>

<p><strong>Vault creation is never silent.</strong></p>

<hr />

<h2>Can I recover my vault password?</h2>

<p><strong>No.</strong>  
There is no recovery mechanism. If you lose your password:</p>

<ol>
  <li>Restore from a backup</li>
  <li>Re-export tokens from providers</li>
  <li>Create a new vault</li>
</ol>

<hr />

<h2>Why does Desktop‑2FA require the password every time?</h2>

<p>For security reasons:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Every vault access requires explicit password input</li>
  <li>Even during the unlock window, password must be provided</li>
  <li><code>.vault-unlocked</code> contains only a timestamp</li>
</ul>

<p>This prevents accidental or unauthorized access.</p>

<hr />

<h2>Why was my old vault rejected?</h2>

<h3>Vault Format Change (0.6.0)</h3>

<p>Vaults created before version 0.6.0 are <strong>not compatible</strong> with current versions.</p>

<p>You will see:</p>

<pre><code>Unsupported vault format</code></pre>

<p>Old vaults lacked:</p>

<ul>
  <li>version field</li>
  <li>magic header <code>D2FA</code></li>
  <li>stable Argon2id parameters</li>
  <li>audited ciphertext layout</li>
</ul>

<p>Create a new vault using:</p>

<pre><code>d2fa init-vault</code></pre>

<hr />

<h2>Why does rename sometimes fail?</h2>

<p>If multiple entries share the same name, rename is aborted:</p>

<pre><code>Error: Multiple entries named '&lt;name&gt;' exist. Operation aborted. Resolve duplicates first.
</code></pre>

<p>This ensures deterministic behavior.</p>

<hr />

<h2>Why does Desktop‑2FA reject my Base32 secret?</h2>

<p>Secrets must be valid Base32.  
If invalid:</p>

<pre><code>[red]Invalid secret: not valid Base32.[/red]
Example: ABCDEFGHIJKL2345
</code></pre>

<p>Fix the secret or re-copy it from the provider.</p>

<hr />

<h2>Does Desktop‑2FA support otpauth:// URLs?</h2>

<p>Yes.</p>

<pre><code>d2fa add "otpauth://totp/GitHub:lukas?secret=ABC123&issuer=GitHub"
</code></pre>

<p>The CLI extracts:</p>

<ul>
  <li>issuer</li>
  <li>label</li>
  <li>secret</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Why does Desktop‑2FA ask for password confirmation?</h2>

<p>Only during vault creation.  
This prevents accidental creation with mistyped passwords.</p>

<hr />

<h2>Why does Desktop‑2FA refuse to run without a password?</h2>

<p>In non‑interactive mode, password must be provided via:</p>

<ul>
  <li><code>--password</code></li>
  <li><code>--password-file</code></li>
</ul>

<p>This prevents scripts from accidentally creating or modifying vaults without authentication.</p>

<hr />

<h2>Can I use Desktop‑2FA in scripts?</h2>

<p>Yes — use:</p>

<pre><code>--password-file
</code></pre>

<p>Ensure the file is readable only by your user.</p>

<hr />

<h2>Why does Desktop‑2FA warn about weak passwords?</h2>

<p>Password strength is configurable:</p>

<pre><code>[security]
min_password_entropy = 60
reject_weak_passwords = false
</code></pre>

<p>Weak passwords may be:</p>

<ul>
  <li>allowed with warning</li>
  <li>rejected</li>
  <li>bypassed with <code>--allow-weak-passwords</code></li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>How do I back up my vault?</h2>

<pre><code>d2fa backup
d2fa export backup.bin
</code></pre>

<p>Backups are encrypted with the same password.</p>

<hr />

<h2>How do I migrate to another machine?</h2>

<pre><code># On source machine
d2fa export transfer.bin

# Transfer file

# On destination machine
d2fa import transfer.bin
</code></pre>

<hr />

<h2>Does Desktop‑2FA sync between devices?</h2>

<p>No.  
It is intentionally offline‑only for security.</p>

<hr />

<h2>Does Desktop‑2FA store anything in the cloud?</h2>

<p>No.  
Everything is local and encrypted.</p>

<hr />

<h2>How do I report issues?</h2>

<p>Contact: <strong>contact@desktop-2fa.org</strong></p>


